---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Services';
const description =
  'Two families of work: building sites that get found, and fixing ones that do not. Audit, fix-it sprint, retainer, and full builds. Each lists what it excludes.';

const service = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  serviceType: 'SEO and answer engine optimisation',
  provider: { '@id': 'https://www.snippettester.com/#org' },
  name: 'Evidence-led SEO and AEO',
  description,
  areaServed: { '@type': 'Place', name: 'Worldwide' },
  hasOfferCatalog: {
    '@type': 'OfferCatalog',
    name: 'Engagements',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'Offer', name: 'Structural audit', price: '750', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
      { '@type': 'Offer', name: 'Fix-it sprint', price: '2400', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
      { '@type': 'Offer', name: 'Ongoing retainer', price: '2900', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    ],
  },
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} schema={[service]}>
  <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Services</p>
      <h1>Two families of work. No packages named after metals.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Either we fix the site you have, or we replace it. Everything below is scoped as deliverables
        you can check off, not hours you cannot verify, and each one lists what it does not include —
        usually the more useful half.
      </p>
      <p>
        Build work lives on its own page: <a href="/websites">how we build websites</a>, and why
        static beats WordPress when the reader is a machine. The three engagements below are for
        sites that already exist.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="stack" style="max-width: 54rem;">

        <div class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">01 · one-off · 5 working days</p>
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem;">Structural audit</h2>
          <span class="price">$750 <small>fixed</small></span>
          <p>
            The full version of what the free checker gestures at, done by hand across your whole
            site rather than one page. You get a document you could hand to any developer.
          </p>
          <h4>You get</h4>
          <ul class="quiet-list">
            <li>Every template audited for structure, schema, heading order and extractability</li>
            <li>Your actual AI Overview exposure measured from Search Console — which queries, how much traffic, informational versus transactional split</li>
            <li>Crawl and rendering check: what a crawler sees versus what a browser sees</li>
            <li>A prioritised fix list, each item with the reasoning and the expected size of effect</li>
            <li>A 45-minute call to argue with the findings</li>
          </ul>
          <h4>Not included</h4>
          <ul class="quiet-list no-list">
            <li>Implementation — this is diagnosis</li>
            <li>Content writing</li>
            <li>Keyword research as a standalone deliverable</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">02 · one-off · 3-4 weeks</p>
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem;">Fix-it sprint</h2>
          <span class="price">$2,400 <small>fixed</small></span>
          <p>
            The audit, then the changes actually made. Most sites do not need an ongoing relationship
            — they need a backlog cleared once by someone who knows which items matter.
          </p>
          <h4>You get</h4>
          <ul class="quiet-list">
            <li>Everything in the audit</li>
            <li>Schema implemented and validated across every template</li>
            <li>Your top 10 pages restructured answer-first: opening rewritten, headings resequenced, key facts moved into tables</li>
            <li>Technical fixes shipped — metadata, canonicals, robots rules, crawl waste, render-blocking</li>
            <li>Before-and-after measurement, and a baseline you can keep tracking without me</li>
          </ul>
          <h4>Not included</h4>
          <ul class="quiet-list no-list">
            <li>New content production at volume</li>
            <li>Link building</li>
            <li>Anything requiring a rebuild of your CMS</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">03 · monthly · 30-day rolling</p>
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem;">Ongoing retainer</h2>
          <span class="price">$2,900 <small>/ month</small></span>
          <p>
            For sites where content ships continuously and the structural work has to keep up. Rolling
            monthly, no minimum term. If a month produces nothing worth reporting I will tell you and
            suggest pausing.
          </p>
          <h4>You get</h4>
          <ul class="quiet-list">
            <li>Four to six pages produced or rebuilt per month, written answer-first</li>
            <li>Citation and visibility monitoring across Google AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Perplexity and Copilot</li>
            <li>Schema maintained as templates change</li>
            <li>A monthly report that leads with what did not work</li>
            <li>Direct access — you are talking to the person doing the work</li>
          </ul>
          <h4>Not included</h4>
          <ul class="quiet-list no-list">
            <li>Paid media</li>
            <li>Social or email marketing</li>
            <li>Guaranteed rankings or guaranteed citations — nobody can honestly sell those</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card" style="border-style: dashed;">
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">04 · when fixing is the wrong answer</p>
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem;">Rebuild</h2>
          <p>
            Some sites cannot be optimised into shape. A client-rendered React site serves crawlers an
            empty shell; a theme with fifteen plugins carries more overhead than content. In those
            cases the honest recommendation is replacement, and the audit fee comes off the build.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/websites">How we build websites →</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="prose" style="margin-top: 3.5rem;">Side by side</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll" style="max-width: 54rem;">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">&nbsp;</th>
              <th scope="col">Audit</th>
              <th scope="col">Sprint</th>
              <th scope="col">Retainer</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Price</th><td class="num">$750</td><td class="num">$2,400</td><td class="num">$2,900/mo</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Turnaround</th><td>5 working days</td><td>3-4 weeks</td><td>Rolling monthly</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Templates audited</th><td>All</td><td>All</td><td>All, re-checked monthly</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">AI Overview exposure measured</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Tracked continuously</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Schema implemented</th><td>No — specified only</td><td>Yes, all templates</td><td>Yes, maintained</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Pages restructured</th><td>0</td><td>10</td><td>4-6 per month</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Citation monitoring</th><td>No</td><td>Baseline only</td><td>4 engines, ongoing</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Minimum term</th><td>n/a</td><td>n/a</td><td>None</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="source-note" style="max-width: 54rem;">
        For context on the rates: AEO retainers surveyed at
        <a href="https://humanswith.ai/blog/what-aeo-and-geo-actually-cost-in-2026/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">$2,000-$15,000 per month in 2026</a>,
        and <a href="https://outreachbloom.com/aeo-geo-pricing-guide/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">anything under $1,500 is generally rebranded SEO</a>.
        The 3-4% AI Overview trigger rate on transactional queries is from
        <a href="https://searchengineland.com/google-ai-overviews-ctr-recovery-study-475566" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">aggregated 2026 trigger-rate analysis</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout" style="max-width: 54rem;">
        <p>
          <strong>Who should not hire me.</strong> If your site scores above 80 on the free checker,
          the remaining structural gains are small and you would be paying me to polish. If your
          traffic is almost entirely transactional queries, AI Overviews trigger on 3-4% of those and
          your exposure is minimal. And if you need volume content production more than structural
          work, a writer is better value than an auditor.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <a class="btn btn--accent" href="/#checker">Start with the free check</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/contact">Ask about an engagement</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
